(chapter
  (slug "using-the-playground")
  (title "The playground")
  (pillar "language")
  (section "Wrapping up")
  (blurb "The full editor: a REPL, compiled-output views, and shareable links.")
  (lede "Every example in this guide is a scratchpad, but the " (app-link (route "/playground") "playground") " is the full workshop: a real editor, a REPL, and a window into what your code compiles to.")
  (p "The same compiler that runs the guide's inline examples powers a standalone editor. Nothing runs on a server: the compiler is itself WebAssembly, so your code is compiled and executed entirely in your browser. Here's what you can do there beyond pressing Run.")
  (h2 "A REPL over your own module")
  (p "Pressing Run evaluates one thing: your module's " (c "main") ". But a module usually defines " (em "many") " functions, and often you want to poke at them one at a time: call " (c "double") " on 21, then on 100, then try " (c "compose") " on the result. That's what the " (strong "REPL") " tab is for.")
  (p "Type an expression, in whichever syntax you're using, and press Enter. It's evaluated against every definition your buffer declares, and the value comes back rendered just like a run. Say your editor holds:")
  (note (cdz (def (double n) (* n 2))) " and " (cdz (def (add a b) (+ a b))))
  (p "Then in the REPL you can enter " (cdz (double 21)) " and get " (c "42") ", or nest calls freely: " (cdz (double (add 3 4))) " gives " (c "14") ". Results aren't limited to numbers: an expression that builds a list or a tuple shows the whole value, the same canonical form a run produces. Press the up arrow to recall a previous expression and tweak it.")
  (note "The REPL calls your functions by compiling a tiny program that invokes them and running it, so a REPL answer is exactly what the compiled program computes, never a separate interpreter that might disagree. It's the real thing, every time.")
  (h2 "See what it compiles to")
  (p "The " (strong "Compiled") " tab shows the output of the backend. " (strong "WAT") " is the WebAssembly text of the core module your program becomes, the actual instructions that run. " (strong "Rust") " (and " (strong "Rust (async)") ") show the same program lowered to Rust source instead, because Cadenza is target-neutral above its backend: one typed program, more than one place it can go. " (strong "Cadenza") " shows the program lowered and optimized back to Cadenza itself: where WAT and Rust are other languages, this stays in the source language, so you read exactly what the optimizer did, the constant-folding, inlining, and match-compilation, as Cadenza. Write " (cdz (+ (* 2 3) n)) " and the Cadenza view shows the " (cdz (* 2 3)) " already folded to " (c "6") ".")
  (p "It's worth a look even if you never write WebAssembly by hand: seeing " (cdz (+ 2 3)) " become a single constant, or a recursive function become a loop, makes the compiler feel less like a black box.")
  (h2 "The editor helps as you type")
  (p "The playground's editor is a small IDE. Mistakes are underlined " (em "as you type") ", with the same diagnostics the compiler would report, with no need to run first. Hover a name to see its inferred type. The " (strong "Diagnostics") " tab lists every problem, and clicking one jumps to it in the source. The " (strong "AST") " tab shows the raw tree the compiler sees, the one structure both syntaxes are just views of.")
  (p "It goes further than underlining. Rest the caret on a name and every other use of it tints softly, the declaration included, so you can see a value's whole reach at a glance. " (strong "⌘-click") " (or Ctrl-click) that name to jump straight to where it's defined. And when a mistake has an obvious repair, the diagnostic offers a " (em "quick-fix") ": apply it and the compiler's suggested edit is spliced in for you. Call a function and the editor writes each argument's name in front of it in soft grey, so " (c "add(1, 2)") " reads as " (c "add(x: 1, y: 2)") " with the parameter names taken from the function's own definition, and an argument already named after its parameter is left alone so the hints stay quiet. And where you leave a parameter's type to be inferred, the editor writes that inferred type in the same soft grey, so " (c "def double(x)") " reads as " (c "def double(x: Int64)") "; a parameter you annotated yourself, or one that stays fully generic, gets no such hint. These aren't editor heuristics guessing from spelling: every one is the compiler answering a question about your actual program, live in the browser.")
  (p "Even the colours come from the compiler. Rather than guessing a token's role from its spelling, the editor asks the compiler what each name " (em "means") ", so a type, a constructor, a function, a local, and an " (em "unbound") " name (a typo the compiler can't resolve) each get their own colour. The highlighting is the same understanding the type checker has, not a separate guess.")
  (h2 "Share what you made")
  (p "Press " (strong "Share") " and the whole program is packed into the page's URL: no account, no server, no saved file. Send the link and the recipient opens your exact program, in the syntax you wrote it. It's the fastest way to ask a question or show something off.")
  (h2 "A calculator, too")
  (p "There's a lighter-weight sibling for quick sums: the " (app-link (route "/calculator") "calculator") ". It's a focused prompt over the same language, tuned for arithmetic you'd otherwise reach for a desk calculator to do, except it's " (em "exact") ". Fractions stay fractions (" (c "1 / 3 + 1 / 3 + 1 / 3") " is " (c "1") ", not " (c "0.9999…") "), dimensioned quantities carry their units, and big integers don't overflow. Recall the previous result with " (c "ans") " and keep going.")
  (p "It's the " (strong "Exact fractions") " and " (strong "Units of measure") " chapters made interactive: reach for it when you want a number now, and the playground when you want a program.")
  (h2 "More to explore")
  (p "Two more surfaces run the same in-browser compiler, each showing off a different corner of the language. The " (link (slug "cad") "CAD preview") " renders a solid modelled in Cadenza, a 3D shape built from exact rational coordinates and real units (the " (strong "exact CAD") " worked example in " (link (slug "units") "Units of measure") " made visible), which you can orbit and inspect. And the " (link (slug "notebook") "notebook") " is a live document: markdown prose interleaved with runnable code cells and interactive widgets: drag a slider and every dependent cell recomputes, so a compound-interest model or a data table stays parametric. Both are ordinary Cadenza programs; they just render its results as geometry, tables, and charts instead of a single value.")
  (h2 "Beyond the browser")
  (p "Everything here runs the real compiler in your browser, and that same compiler ships as a single command-line tool for working on your own machine, which the next chapter, " (link (slug "cdz-toolchain") "The cdz toolchain") ", covers in full.")
  (p "For now, open the " (app-link (route "/playground") "playground") " and paste in the last program you wrote in this guide, then reach for the REPL and start calling its pieces."))
